# DTF tier manifest: sbom-license-expression-trivy   ADVISORY (needs internet)
#   (artifact-keeper#5629, 1.12.4)
# Profile-set: filesystem + scanners.trivy (the scanner adapter, image
# $SCANNER_ADAPTER_IMAGE), so the backend registers the Trivy filesystem
# scanner on the adapter beside its bundled Grype. The adapter downloads the
# Trivy DB from TRIVY_DB_REPOSITORY on its first scan (warmed in setup).
#
# THE BUG
# #5629  With the Trivy filesystem scanner enabled (a standard Helm install
#        has one), a package-lock.json entry licensed `MIT AND PSF-2.0` came
#        out of the CycloneDX SBOM as `license.name:
#        LicenseRef-MIT-AND-PSF-2.0` instead of `{"expression": ...}` (#3866),
#        and `Public Domain` as `LicenseRef-Public-Domain`. Both scanners
#        catalog the package: Trivy stores a license it cannot read as one
#        SPDX id wrapped as `LicenseRef-<sanitised>` (#1152), Grype stores the
#        text. The SBOM read kept whichever row the query returned first, and
#        `LicenseRef-...` sorts before `MIT ...` and `Public ...`.
#        `Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception` sorts first and survived. Fixed: a
#        LicenseRef that is exactly the wrapped form of the other scanner's
#        text gives way to that text. Licenses that differ are never swapped.
#
# #6379  (1.13.0) A Trivy license LIST that joins a compound expression with
#        another license, ["MIT AND PSF-2.0", "ISC"] (a composer.lock array),
#        was stored as `LicenseRef-MIT-AND-PSF-2.0 AND ISC`: an expression
#        with an opaque operand, which the #5629 swap (one wrapped term only)
#        cannot repair. Fixed: an element that is already a conjunctive SPDX
#        expression (AND / WITH, no OR) is kept as written, so the row is
#        `MIT AND LicenseRef-PSF-2.0 AND ISC` (PSF-2.0 is outside the
#        recognised id list and is wrapped alone). An element with OR stays wrapped (#1152).
#
# THE GATES
#   (CONTROL) every image: both scans complete; the Trivy filesystem scan
#     stored dtf-compound as LicenseRef-MIT-AND-PSF-2.0 and Grype as the
#     text (without both rows the FIX case proves nothing); a single SPDX id
#     stays license.id and `Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception` is an expression.
#   (FIX) #5629: dtf-compound is {"expression":"MIT AND PSF-2.0"} and dtf-free
#     is {"license":{"name":"Public Domain"}}. RED before the fix.
#
#   (CONTROL) #6379: dtf/choice (["MIT OR Apache-2.0", "ISC"]) is stored as
#     `LicenseRef-MIT-OR-Apache-2.0 AND ISC` on every image.
#   (FIX) #6379: the Trivy filesystem scan stores dtf/joined as
#     `MIT AND LicenseRef-PSF-2.0 AND ISC` (main: `LicenseRef-MIT-AND-PSF-2.0
#     AND ISC`) and its SBOM licenses never carry the compound as one
#     LicenseRef. RED before the fix.
#     Grype stores this entry as `ISC` alone and the SBOM keeps whichever
#     row sorts first, so the SBOM entry may be `ISC` (a separate gap).
#   Since #6379 Trivy stores dtf-compound as `MIT AND LicenseRef-PSF-2.0`; the
#   first CONTROL accepts either form, and the #5629 swap keeps Grype's text.
#
# The sbom-license-expression tier (dtf-gate) gates #3866 with Grype alone;
# it has no adapter, which is why it never saw this.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: requests back to back.
COVERS="5629 6379"
PROFILES="storage.filesystem scanners.trivy"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
NEEDS_INTERNET="true"
